   {align:center} in an article source wraps its blocks in one of these.
   The descendant selector is deliberate: text-align is inherited, but a
   component that declares its own beats what it inherits, so reaching the
   children is the only way an author's local choice can win over the
   theme -- which is what an instance tag is for.

   The class is DOUBLED on that arm, and the reason is measured. A plain
   `.align-x *` is (0,1,0), which loses to `.full-article p` and
   `.comparison-table td` at (0,1,1) -- so the tag was inert on exactly the
   long-form prose it exists for, while its `hyphens` companion (which had
   no competitor) still landed: a paragraph came out left-aligned AND
   hyphenated, which no layer asked for. Doubling reaches (0,2,0).
   These arms set alignment and nothing else. Breaking words at end of
   line is its own axis (page.hyphens), off unless asked for by name, so
   re-aligning a block never changes whether its words break. */
.align-left,    .align-left.align-left *       { text-align: left; }
.align-center,  .align-center.align-center *   { text-align: center; }
.align-right,   .align-right.align-right *     { text-align: right; }
.align-justify, .align-justify.align-justify * { text-align: justify; }

/* --- Short viewports: a phone held sideways ---
   375px of height with 120px of fixed padding leaves no room for a title,
   a summary and a fact box, and no type scale fixes that. Reclaiming the
   padding takes the landscape phones from 6 overflowing cards in 8 down to
   3; the rest degrades gracefully, since .slide is min-height, so a card
   that will not fit grows and the page scrolls. Inert on every other
   viewport -- portrait and desktop are unchanged to the pixel. */
@media (max-height: 520px) {
  .slide { padding: 24px 6vw; }
  .fact-box { padding: 14px 18px; }
  .highlight { margin: 8px 0 10px; }
}
