  --theme name           install/set-theme: apply a named palette (see THEMES
                        below); unknown name is a fatal error
  --polarity light|dark  themes: list only themes with that background polarity
  --intensity name       themes: sober | vivid | mono
  --hue name             themes: neutral, red, orange, yellow, green, cyan,
                        blue, violet, magenta — the hue of the page background
  --gitlab-ci             install: also write a .gitlab-ci.yml pipeline (opt-in —
                        install never assumes a GitLab deployment on its own)
  --language-file path   build/check: use this language pack file instead of
                        language/<lang>.json (highest priority, spec §19.5)
  --no-typography        build/check: disables ALL typography rules (non-breaking
                        spaces included) for this run — no rule runs anywhere,
                        not just the ones below (see TYPOGRAPHY section)
  --only file             build: rebuild just this one article (by page_dest or
                        page_source name) instead of the whole series, IF
                        nothing that affects index.html/series-nav changed
                        since the last build (checked via --nav-cache) —
                        silently falls back to a full build otherwise, never
                        a stale page (spec §11.3.1)
  --scaffold              refresh-templates: regenerate settings.conf's commented
                        surface at the current theme's values, keeping your
                        pinned (uncommented) lines — the one case where the
                        tool rewrites that file (spec §9.4.3)
  --include-drafts        build/check: also build articles marked draft: true
                        (excluded entirely by default — no page, no index
                        card, no nav entry). Draft pages built this way carry
                        a centered "draft" banner in their header so a
                        preview is never mistaken for a publication.
  --nav-cache path        build --only: where the nav/index fingerprint used for
                        that safety check is read from and written to
                        (default: .lwp-cache/nav.json in the series directory)
  --build-stamp           build: stamps every generated page with a discreet
                        "Compiled at <date/time> with lightwebpres v<version>"
                        marker, in the page's own header (visible only while
                        scrolled to the top) — a live timestamp, so off by
                        default and ignored by check's comparison either way
                        (spec §11.3.2)
  --build-stamp-minimal   Same marker, without the date/time or version —
                        "Compiled with lightwebpres." — for when even the
                        build date isn't something you want published.
                        Wins over --build-stamp if both are passed.

THEMES ({theme_count} built in, see specifications.md §9.5)
  Themes are chosen by slug, and found by facet — polarity (light/dark),
  intensity (sober/vivid/mono), and hue, computed from the background:
    lightwebpres themes                            all {theme_count}, with their facets
    lightwebpres themes --polarity dark --intensity sober  just the ones you mean
  Then apply one, at scaffold time or afterwards:
    lightwebpres install my-series --theme evergreen
    lightwebpres set-theme my-series --theme evergreen
  set-theme rewrites the one `theme:` line of templates/settings.conf and
  nothing else: your uncommented values are untouched and still apply on
  top of the new theme. The stylesheet is composed at build time, so
  upgrading the executable never silently reverts anything.
  Preview every theme rendered against real slide content, with facet
  filters, at:
    lightwebpres themes-gallery

SETTINGS (templates/settings.conf, see specifications.md §9)
  Every visual decision is a typed property, `component.axis: value`, and
  settings.conf lists ALL of them, commented out at the current theme's
  values. Uncomment a line to pin it: it survives every theme change and
  every executable upgrade, and lightwebpres never rewrites your file
  (set-theme touches only the `theme:` line).
  Shared values themes provide, referenced by bare words:
    color.page    the page background
    color.ink     body text          color.ink-quiet   secondary text
    color.mark    what marks         color.call        what calls attention
    color.affirm  what affirms       (the "yes" verdict — posed as text)
    font.text / font.display / font.ui / font.mono
                  four stacks; each must end on a CSS generic family
  Component properties name what they paint — a few of the {property_count}:
    verdict.partial.fg: #8a4b00     one verdict, nothing else moves
    tag.transform: none             tags stop being uppercase
    cover.bg.to: #44475A            the cover becomes a gradient
    title1.shadow.fg: #33FF8866     a halo (a shadow with no offset)
    fact.strong.weight: normal      un-bold the fact-box emphasis
  A bare word is looked up in its type's namespace (`tag.fg: ink-quiet`);
  a dotted word references another property (`title1.fg: cover.fg`).
  Errors name the file and the key — a mistyped property is a build error,
  never a silent no-op. CSS rules (new selectors, @font-face, media
  queries) belong in templates/custom.css, appended after the composed
  stylesheet so your rules win ties.

ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
  LWP_ARTICLES_DIR    Articles directory (default: $LWP_SERIES_DIR/articles)
  LWP_TEMPLATES_DIR   Templates directory (default: $LWP_SERIES_DIR/templates)
  LWP_OUTPUT_DIR      Output directory (default: $LWP_SERIES_DIR/public)
  LWP_LANG            Language (default: fr)
  LWP_SERIES_DIR      Series directory (default: .) — used when no
                      [directory] argument is given
  LWP_LANGUAGE_DIR    Language directory (default: $LWP_SERIES_DIR/language)

SERIES DIRECTORY (created by install)
  series.json          List of articles + series metadata
  articles/*.md         One .md file per article (+ *_article.md for the full article)
  articles/img/          Article images (copied to public/img/ on build)
  templates/            settings.conf (your pinned values), custom.css (your
                        rules), nav.js (page/index HTML structure is fixed,
                        not a template — see language/ for vocabulary)
  language/              fr.json, en.json — typography rules + interface
                        strings (nav labels, "copy link" button...), editable;
                        strings can be partially overridden (missing keys
                        fall back to the built-in pack for that language)
  public/               Generated HTML (build output — do not edit by hand)
  README.md              Generated by build from series.json (article list)
  lightwebpres            Copy of the executable itself, for standalone use
  .lwp-cache/nav.json     Written by build, read by build --only (spec §11.3.1) —
                        derived state, safe to delete, never edited by hand

ARTICLE FORMAT (.md)
  <!-- lwp:meta --> ... ---     Metadata block at the top of the file
  ---                           Separates slides, alone on its own line
  <!-- lwp:slide:TYPE -->       Marks a slide's type (absent = standard)
  key: value                    A slide field (one line = one value; no line
                                breaks inside a value for now)

SLIDE TYPES
  cover         Cover slide (tag, # Title, summary)
  standard      Regular slide (tag, ## Title, summary, highlight,
                highlight-caption, fact-label, fact-variant, source,
                + free text = fact-box)
  series-nav    Cross-article navigation block, generated from series.json
  full-article  Includes an external .md file via the article: field
                (required; only one full-article slide per article)

FIELDS RECOGNIZED IN A SLIDE
  tag, ## Title, summary, fact-label, fact-variant, source, highlight,
  highlight-caption, comment (review note, never rendered)

ARTICLE-LEVEL FIELDS (series.json entry, overriding the .md meta block)
  page_source (required, series.json only), page_dest, page_title, page_desc,
  card_title, card_desc, card_label, nav_title, nav_desc, author, license,
  date, draft, notes-placement, notes-tooltip, comment — see GLOSSARY.md
  for every field's scope and
  fallback chain. author/license default from series_meta for the whole
  series; author/date/license are displayed (page footer + <meta> tags),
  page_desc feeds <meta name="description">.

NOTES (specifications.md §6.5)
  [^label] calls a note, [^label]: body defines it — standard Markdown, in
  a slide's free text or in the full-article file. The call links to the
  body, the body links back, and the label never reaches the page: what
  the reader sees is a position, so inserting a note renumbers nothing you
  wrote.

  Two article-level <!-- lwp:meta --> fields, also settable in series_meta
  (the article wins):
    notes-placement: local   bodies at the foot of the unit that called
                             them — that card, or the end of the long-form
                             article. Numbering restarts in each card,
                             because a card is shareable on its own. Default.
    notes-placement: page    every body collected into one notes section at
                             the end of the page, numbered continuously
    notes-tooltip: on        also put the body's text on the call. Composes
                             with either placement, never the only carrier
                             (the body stays in the document). Default off.
  An unknown value is a build error naming the article.

  audit names three things, none of them fatal: a call with no body, a
  body nothing calls, and a body written inside a raw HTML block (raw HTML
  is passed through verbatim, so it ships as literal text).

TYPOGRAPHY (non-breaking spaces) — this alters generated content, see specifications.md §7/§19
  The built-in French pack (language/fr.json) inserts a non-breaking space
  automatically in generated HTML: before ; : ! ? and %, after an opening «,
  between groups of 3 digits in a number the source already spaces out
  (e.g. "170 000"), between a number and million(s)/milliard(s)/dollar(s)/$,
  and after × or ≈ before a number. It only ever upgrades a space the
  source already has to a non-breaking one — it never inserts spacing or
  digit grouping that wasn't there, and a non-breaking space already in
  the source is always left exactly as written.

  Three article-level <!-- lwp:meta --> fields turn part or all of this
  off, per article, for its own page only:
    typo-units: off       disables the number/unit and ×|≈/number rules
    typo-thousands: off   disables the thousands-grouping rule
    typo: off             disables every rule for this article, not just
                          the two above (same effect as --no-typography,
                          scoped to one article)
  Any other value, or the field's absence, leaves the rule on (default).

  --no-typography (build/check) disables the whole engine for that run —
  every rule, on every article, not just the fields above — for when you
  need the generated HTML to match the Markdown source's spacing exactly.

The framework is a single executable file, with no external dependency.
Python 3.8+ standard library only. Native UTF-8. On Windows
(no shebang), run: python lightwebpres <command>.
